      "episode_id": "VR-488506",
      "has_more": false,
      "messages": [
        {
          "content": "Juror-2 arithmetic check of INV-2207. Every line total is internally correct: 6 x 24.50 = 147.00; 200 x 0.45 = 90.00; 2 x 38.00 = 76.00; 3 x 12.80 = 38.40; 1 x 15.00 = 15.00. But those line totals sum to 366.40, not the stated subtotal of 376.40 - a 10.00 discrepancy. The VAT and total are computed from the stated subtotal and are self-consistent with it (376.40 x 20% = 75.28; 376.40 + 75.28 = 451.68); on the actual sum of the line items, 20% VAT would be 73.28 and the total 439.68. So the claim as worded - that 451.68 equals the sum of the line items plus 20% VAT - does not hold: 451.68 equals the stated subtotal plus 20% VAT, and the stated subtotal is 10.00 more than the line items add up to. Reading 'sum of its line items' as the printed line totals, the shortfall is 12.00 against 439.68. Uncertainties: whether a line or charge was omitted from the invoice is not something the document shows; the arithmetic discrepancy itself is not in doubt.",
          "content_hash": "0x8342b71d0fe61cc7aa639111f0e3c6c7f49387abf28116d4b7b30e74d4e6037d",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321052939,
          "message_id": "7f632711-e958-4df2-a1e3-2667f3e20ae6",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-2",
          "sequence": 1
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-3 arithmetic check on INV-2207 (Harbour Packaging, 2 Oct 2026). The five line totals are each internally correct: 6 x 24.50 = 147.00; 200 x 0.45 = 90.00; 2 x 38.00 = 76.00; 3 x 12.80 = 38.40; 1 x 15.00 = 15.00. Their sum is 147.00 + 90.00 + 76.00 + 38.40 + 15.00 = 366.40. The invoice states a subtotal of 376.40, which is 10.00 more than the sum of its own line items, and then computes VAT of 75.28 and a total of 451.68 consistently from that overstated subtotal (376.40 x 1.20 = 451.68). The claim as published is that 451.68 equals the sum of the line items plus 20% VAT. It does not: 366.40 + 20% = 73.28 VAT, giving 439.68, which is 12.00 less than 451.68. The gap is the same under other readings I tried: excluding the delivery charge gives 351.40 + 20% = 421.68, and applying 20% to the stated subtotal only reproduces the 451.68 figure by first accepting the 10.00 subtotal error. Uncertainties: the only document in the case is the invoice as reproduced; if a further line item (for example a 10.00 surcharge) exists on the original document but is missing from this record, the subtotal would reconcile, though no such line appears here. On the record as submitted the stated total does not equal the sum of the line items plus 20% VAT.",
          "content_hash": "0xf34ee0a82a2acc1b88e9d56c280d597d3b68dab9f9445e9ec1d6eac675af9633",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321057106,
          "message_id": "2a19803a-1b57-41b6-ad40-c62976c6b427",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-3",
          "sequence": 2
        },
        {
          "content": "Juror-1 independent arithmetic check of INV-2207. Each line recomputes correctly: 6 x 24.50 = 147.00; 200 x 0.45 = 90.00; 2 x 38.00 = 76.00; 3 x 12.80 = 38.40; 1 x 15.00 = 15.00. Sum of the line items = 366.40. 20% VAT on that = 73.28, so line items plus 20% VAT = 439.68. The invoice instead states a subtotal of 376.40 (10.00 more than the line items sum to) and a total due of 451.68, which is 12.00 more than 439.68. So on the face of the document the stated total does not equal the sum of its line items plus 20% VAT; the discrepancy comes from the stated subtotal, not from the VAT rate. Robustness: if the 15.00 delivery charge is excluded from 'line items', the figure is 351.40 + 70.28 = 421.68, further from the stated total, so the conclusion is the same on either reading. Uncertainty: this is a single document and no purchase order, credit note, or revised invoice is in the case; a missing line item or a corrected invoice would change the picture.",
          "content_hash": "0x6e61d17f9e2c24fa98c3fe11907653d3c31bfe5ad729813190407413441faf1d",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321061407,
          "message_id": "985c3471-60e9-47c4-8030-d8eaad55cd37",
          "round_num": 1,
          "sender_id": "juror-1",
          "sequence": 3
        },
        {
          "content": "Challenged. INV-2207's five line totals each recompute correctly, but they sum to 366.40 (147.00 + 90.00 + 76.00 + 38.40 + 15.00), not the 376.40 subtotal the invoice states - a 10.00 overstatement. The VAT and total are self-consistent with the wrong subtotal: 376.40 x 20% = 75.28 and 376.40 + 75.28 = 451.68. Correctly computed, the line items plus 20% VAT are 366.40 + 73.28 = 439.68, so the stated 451.68 total exceeds its own line items plus VAT by 12.00. Equivalently, 451.68 implies a base of 376.40, which is 10.00 more than the line items sum to. Excluding delivery (line 5, 15.00) does not rescue it: 351.40 + 20% = 421.68. The claim that the 451.68 total equals the line items plus 20% VAT therefore does not hold. Uncertainty: the invoice document is the only evidence in the case, so this rests on the line items and figures as printed.",
          "content_hash": "0x855eb04d4b517537ffdb90082ac9d1dcc0eb82f55bcffc074dd8009d33cd1d54",
          "created_at_ms": 1793321160327,
          "message_id": "d9e70cdb-2dd8-4007-bb14-422a220035ee",
          "round_num": null,
          "sender_id": "challenger",
          "sequence": 4
        }
      ],
      "next_after": 4
    }
